Labral MRI findings without symptoms — Schwartzberg and colleagues (@med-source-asymptomatic-labrum: 0, is #reference, #part-of @medicine-sources) { r_7E5C296ZF4P7MWT3H5B0NXV6H1
Randy Schwartzberg, Bryan L. Reuss, Bradd G. Burkhart, Matt Butterfield, James Y. Wu, and Kevin W. McLean. High Prevalence of Superior Labral Tears Diagnosed by MRI in Middle-Aged Patients With Asymptomatic Shoulders. Orthopaedic Journal of Sports Medicine 4, 2016, article 2325967115623212. DOI 10.1177/2325967115623212. English.

[Free full text](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4710128/) · [PubMed record](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26779556/) · [publisher](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/2325967115623212).

Read Methods, Results, and Discussion for the selected imaging exercise. The study examined asymptomatic adults aged 45–60 and compared image interpretations. Its relevance is the distinction between an imaging interpretation and a symptomatic diagnosis. The findings do not establish that every labral tear is harmless or that a younger person, traumatic injury, or symptomatic patient has the same probabilities.

Read the complete article for deeper appraisal of selection, interpretation, and generalizability. Do not apply an isolated prevalence estimate to a different clinical setting.

